# Build context for the validation image (Dockerfile.validate).
#
# Two jobs here, and the second one matters more than the first.
#
# 1. Keep the context small — node_modules and .next are the bulk of the tree
#    and both are rebuilt inside the image anyway.
# 2. Keep secrets out of it. A build context is not a mount: everything listed
#    below would be baked into an image layer and would survive in that layer
#    even if a later step deleted it. `.env.local` holds the InsForge keys and
#    `.vercel`/`.insforge` hold project credentials, so none of them cross this
#    line.
#
# Note what is deliberately NOT excluded: `docs/`, `*.md` and every config file
# at the root. The image runs `prettier --check` and `markdownlint-cli2`, and
# those lint the documentation — excluding it would make two of the gates pass
# by having nothing to look at.

# Dependencies and build output — reinstalled and rebuilt in the image
node_modules
.next
out
dist
build
coverage
playwright-report
test-results
.turbo
.swc

# Secrets and provider state — never in a layer
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.vercel
.insforge
*.pem
*.key

# Local-only tooling and VCS
.git
.husky
.vscode
.idea
.claude
.agents
.codegraph
.docgraph
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store

# The image's own definition
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
